{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>He may not make the son of the beloved the firstborn</b> — a warning not to do this. So too all similar cases: <i>you may not eat</i> within your gates (Deuteronomy 12:17), <i>you may not set over yourself a foreign man</i> (Deuteronomy 17:15). And Onkelos renders all of them (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 21:16): לֵית לָךְ רְשׁוּ (you have no authority). And the reason is: do not give yourself the ability to do such a thing — and this constitutes a strong admonition. Likewise, <i>for he must acknowledge the firstborn, the son of the hated, to give him a double portion</i> — a positive commandment that he must bequeath him a double portion. Now, one who equalizes the firstborn with his brothers in the inheritance transgresses a negative commandment and a positive commandment, even though his words are not legally effective, and all the more so if he did not wish to publicize that he is the firstborn and claimed he is an ordinary son, and bequeathed him as one of his sons, knowing that he is the firstborn — he transgresses both of them. And these are newly disclosed commandments. And from the fact that Scripture says <b>before the face of the son of the hated, the firstborn</b>, it appears to me that this commandment and this law apply only during the lifetime of the firstborn. But if the firstborn died during his father's lifetime, even though he inherits his double portion in the grave and transmits it by law to his sons, if the old man wished and said: My sons shall inherit thus and so in my property, and the sons of my firstborn son shall take thus and so in my property — his words stand, in the same manner that they stand where there is no firstborn. Likewise, he does not transgress this negative commandment if he did not acknowledge the firstborn after his death, for I have not found <b>עַל פְּנֵי</b> except during one's lifetime: <i>before the face of Aaron their father</i> (Numbers 3:4), <i>before the face of Terah</i> (Genesis 11:28), and so all of them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The word פָּשׁוּט here means an ordinary (non-firstborn) son; the phrase ואע״פ שֶׁאֵין דְּבָרָיו קַיָּמִים בַּדִּין means the father's attempted disposition has no legal force (i.e., the firstborn still takes his double portion by Torah law)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:10.595415091Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}